   This paragraph provides background on k-sets. It's a recall of existing concepts and their applications. It's background and motivation, so it's less core.

 Paragraph 3:
   "An interesting observation that can be made on the optimal solution QRhyperplane is that it separates the npoints into τnand(1−τ)npoints [24]. While there are many hyperplanes that separatenpoints into two parts (with τnand(1−τ)npoints), we are interested in the specific hyperplane that provides the lowest optimization score. As k-sets separate the points into kandn−k points, the optimal hyperplane must be one of the k-set separating hyperplanes such that k=τn. The enumeration of all k-sets can also be viewed as a walk along the k-level of an arrangement. Hence, the optimal hyperplane can be found along the vertices encountered during the walk on the k-level of the arrangement."
   This paragraph makes a key observation and connects the problem to k-sets and the k-level arrangement. It provides the theoretical foundation for why the algorithm works. This is a core contribution: it explains the problem formulation and the theoretical insight that leads to the algorithm.

 Paragraph 4:
   "The QR problem in 2 dimensions can utilize this property (the result of Theorem 2). The optimal hyperplane H* in primal space is represented by a point F(H*) in dual space. This point is one of the vertices in the complete skeleton graph corresponding to the k-level arrangement in the dual space. One approach to solving this problem would be to enumerate the vertices corresponding to the k-level of the arrangement and, for each vertex, compute the value of the optimization function. We first present QR2D-k-Set-Baseline algorithm that makes use of the concept of k-sets without the use of aggregate entities to solve the problem. This approach has to compute the value (error) of the optimization function for a vertex that corresponds to a dual point p in the k-level arrangement. Computing the optimization function for each of these vertices involves aggregating the error contribution of each of the n points in the primal space using the line F^{-1}(p). Note that each of the computations consumes O(n) time. For each of the vertices corresponding to the dual points in the k-level arrangement, the naive error computation is repeated, and the optimal line l* corresponds to that line F^{-1}(p) with the least error."
   This paragraph describes the algorithm in detail and the computational steps. It is the core of the method: the algorithm itself and the computational steps. This is a major contribution.

 Paragraph 5:
   "Time complexity : For a given vertex p in the k-level arrangement, a naive approach to compute the error without making use of UpdateNeighbor subroutine takes a total of O(n) time. The upper bound on the intersection points in k-level of an arrangement is bounded by O(n k^{1/3}). Hence, the total time taken for the naive approach is O(n^2 k^{1/3}) for a given τ (k=τn). As k=n/2 when τ= 1/2, the worst case time complexity is O(n^{7/3})."
   This paragraph provides the time complexity analysis. It is a critical part of the scientific contribution because it quantifies the efficiency of the algorithm. However, note that the parent segment is about the algorithm, and the time complexity is a key aspect of the algorithm's performance. But it is a specific analysis and might be considered as part of the algorithm description.

